re --glob '*cc3*' --glob '*.md' | head -90\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"## exception-r3-impl...origin/exception-r3-impl\\\\n691e9bd6 test(yang.clojure): record depth evidence at port tip\\\\ntest/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:944:        (and (seq? x) (= 'yin.k/outcome-of (first x)))\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2017:          ;; (yin.k/outcome-of (f x)): the call's outcome as a value, not unwrapped\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794519755000-engineer-post-port-oracle-reverification.prompt.md:21:cc3 landed the oracle site→C1 occurrence-key binding on the PRE-port shape of `yang/clojure.cljc` (outcome channel). The port has now converged yang.clojure's exception lowering onto the continuation encoding (the owner's directive; Phases 1-4, `8e1dab6d`→`35d09aaa`: continuation family in the clj prelude, consumer wiring, outcome machinery retired net −418 lines, coverage preservation restored, test updates). The port's design preserved the **intra-unit private tail lane** (deepseek's remedy: direct intra-unit calls stay direct tail application; only published/cross-unit entries take a bridge frame) — and that preserved tail lane is exactly the shape cc3's binding targets. The steward's depth measurements cleared the thresholds (155/155 overlap, max ratio 1.20×, max depth 28 frames; `test/yang/latte_oracle/depth_comparison.md`), so budget work is not at risk. This round proves the **binding** still holds.\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md:60:| Trace/budget | `stream/make` 1000000; 2M-bound precedent | restated capacity + instruction bound for the added field |\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794405917000-engineer-cc3-fix-r8.prompt.md:6:# Task: cc3 fix round r8 — make failed retention observable (astra's F1) + the targeted regression (F4)\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794405917000-engineer-cc3-fix-r8.prompt.md:19:1. **F1 P1 — failed retention must be OBSERVABLE, not swallowed** (repl.cljc ~:1510): the r7 blanket catch restores program execution (correct) but produces an ordinary successful value with no provenance handle and NO diagnostic — and the indexer's Warning cannot report failures it never saw (index.cljc:549 consults only indexer state; note the indexer runs BEFORE retention, ~:2021, so a store can publish the index then refuse the provenance relation or record write ~:239; provenance-validation refusals are hidden the same way). **Required fix (astra's words, as spec)**: preserve program execution while retaining an admission-scoped failure diagnostic and making retention-only failures observable. Distinguish failed retention from an unclaimed admission. Concretely: catch around retention, keep the program running, but record the failure (which admission, which stage — relation write vs record write vs validation refusal) somewhere observable — e.g. the round's Warning surface (a second Warning line, or an extended Warning the index-test contract tolerates: read the existing test contracts first and extend them deliberately if needed, stating the contract change) or the session state `repl/call-provenance-handles` shape (a failure entry beside handles). No handle may be advertised for a failed retention; earlier completed handles survive; replay semantics unchanged.\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794403932000-engineer-cc3-fix-r7.muse-wonder-1.0.findings.md:7:**Mechanism (named).** cc3 r2 item 4 connected provenance retention to the production load path: `run-evaluator-stage` in `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc` wraps every loader with `retaining-load`, which — before calling the real `load-program` — calls `retain-packet-call-provenance!`, which calls `jing/materialize!` twice on the session's `:index-store`. Ordinary Clojure rounds do create a claim (verified by probe: evaluating `(defn g …)` then `(g 10)` populates `repl/call-provenance-handles`), so on a store whose `put-bytes-fn` throws, `materialize!` threw *inside the loader*, before the program ever ran. `run-evaluator-stage` propagated it, `eval-program`'s catch routed it to `consume-failed-round`, and the round rendered `Error: store refused the write` with the value (`3`, `5`) lost and the query path's `(:yin.repl.query/index-unavailable)` refusal never produced. At master there is no retention on this path: the program evaluates, and the publication failure is reported by the indexer's own stage as the trailing `Warning:\n58:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_28\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc | sed -n '837,982p;1170,1235p;2195,2265p;2408,2473p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   837\\t\\n   838\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   839\\t;; The continuation lane (slice 5, ported off the outcome model)\\n   840\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   841\\t;;\\n   842\\t;; Opted in with `:outcomes true` beside the argument-vector convention (the\\n   843\\t;; flag now selects the continuation lane, not the retired outcome tuples).\\n   844\\t;; Guest functions return plain values; `throw` lowers to `clj/throw`, `try`\\n   845\\t;; to `clj/try`/`clj/try-finally` (the `%capture` handler-stack family), and a\\n   846\\t;; guest call delivers an escaping condition through the §8.10.7 raise exit.\\n   847\\t;; The public entry installs the same bridge before argument binding. Only the\\n   848\\t;; `compile-program` boundary still wraps the program in `[:yin.k/ok value]` /\\n   849\\t;; `[:yin.k/error raised]` for the harness. `ex-info` becomes a portable map\\n   850\\t;; under namespaced keys, `ex-message`/`ex-data` its accessors. Generated names\\n   851\\t;; carry the reserved `dsg__` prefix; the program pre-pass keeps user code out\\n   852\\t;; of that prefi