e\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-11-13 00:30:44 GMT  \\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-13 07:30:44 +07 (+0700)\\n\\n**Ruling: resolve fixture-side, with a separately justified validation ceiling of 500,000 visits. No owner decision is required.**\\n\\nEvidence below refers to `exception-r3-impl@188702683b272bbd74e42d97a0e5ad3a5f00861b`. Read-only, in-memory verification passed **all 14 existing assertions**, using the real validator with the proposed ceiling.\\n\\n1. **Re-expression is faithful—but transport the existing guest closures, not instructions to recreate them.**\\n\\n   Slice8’s stated scope is immutable kernel state and explicit extension installation. Its reconstruction test requires a registered guest closure capturing `offset=4`, UCF/CBOR reconstruction, and results of `7` from both restored and original machines. It contains no pending user catch or finalizer whose dynamic scope must survive transport. See [slice8 scope and context composition](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:873) and [the complete reconstruction assertions](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1288).\\n\\n   The distinction is between the **kernel context value** and its surrounding **exception runtime**. The existing kernel context survives transport unchanged once construction finishes and the runtime is retired. The original checkpoint instead occurs inside the compiler’s top-level `clj/try`, which is installed unconditionally for this compilation lane: [top boundary](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2203), [boundary application](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2283).\\n\\n   UCF supports closures and reified continuations, but explicitly refuses heap-cell references: [closure/continuation encoding and cell refusal](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1503). My original-checkpoint probe reproduced `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind `:cell`, hint `:cell-1`; that cell held `:re-entered`. Merely unwinding handlers is insufficient: UCF also traverses the root store, including the remaining `clj.rt/ctx` cell reference. See [store traversal](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1634).\\n\\n   This is consistent with the donors:\\n\\n   | Donor | Dynamic context | Handler machinery |\\n   |---|---|---|\\n   | Python | `:handlers :depth :base :frame` | Captured handler continuation and mutable re-entry flag: [context](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:250), [try](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:316) |\\n   | JS | `:handlers` | Same continuation/flag arrangement: [context and try](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1763) |\\n   | PHP | `:handlers :depth :base :frame` | Same continuation/flag arrangement: [context](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:168), [try](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:258) |\\n\\n   Thus convergence exposes an existing transport restriction shared by this runtime family. The outcome fixture previously avoided that restriction; it was not secretly transporting an equivalent active exception handler.\\n\\n2. **The bound belongs to the oracle fixture; set it to 500,000.**\\n\\n   The real recursive validator returned `true` after **449,999 visits** for the original context. The successful transport variant retained the original construction expression and measured **449,999 visits before and after reconstruction**.\\n\\n   `machine-data?` recursively revisits closure payloads and collection members without memoization: [validator](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:938). The compiler explicitly recognizes captured-environment duplication as a validation-cost concern: [definition sequencing](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2231).\\n\\n   This establishes representation-dependent overhead, not a mathematical minimum for continuation encodings. The current representation legitimately passes structural validation but exceeds this fixture’s allowance.\\n\\n   The 100,000 ceiling is supplied only by this oracle’s helper and two call sites; it is not VM-wide machine-data policy: [helper](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1271), [limits](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1326). Its owner is the **LaTTe oracle acceptance-test maintainer**. A fixed **500,000** ceiling gives approximately **11.1% headroom** over the measured value while preserving a finite regression guard. Keep the recursive validator, validity assertion, visit assertion, and exhaustion throw intact. cc3’s retained-byte ruling does not authorize this change.\\n\\n3. **The directive-versus-gate escalation is not triggered.**\\n\\n   The successful variant preserves the captured registry and every existing reconstruction assertion while using the converged exception machinery on both sides. It satisfies the owner’s [continuation directive and no-VM constraint](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/exception-track/1794372000000-owner-decision-r3-vm-retained-seam.md:90).\\n\\n   This ruling establishes proof-context migration across a quiescent runtime boundary. It does not establish active-handler migration.\\n\\n4. **Exact instruction for the next implementer round.**\\n\\n   Work from `18870268`; retain upstream LaTTe pin `86fca0d1d054af005cb37395eae04500d45e6279`. Edit **only** [the reconstruction test and its fixture-local helpers](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1271).\\n\\n   Construct a fixture-local UAST driver with this sequence:\\n\\n   ```text\\n   build the original context under normal compile-program lower\n